SUMMARY
================================================================================

PERFORMANCE (avg cycles)
------------------------
  Results are MIXED — neither configuration wins consistently across all
  45 measurements (5 modes x 3 security levels x 3 operations).

  Overall delta range : -6.38% .. +10.14%
  Overall mean delta  : +0.19%  (essentially a statistical tie at the top level)

  Per-mode breakdown:

    SPEED           : -Os is SLOWER than ATTR in all 9 measurements
                      Mean: +2.2%  |  Range: +1.58% .. +2.80%

    SPEED_DUALCORE  : -Os is FASTER for ML-KEM-512 and ML-KEM-1024,
                      near-parity for ML-KEM-768.
                      Mean: -1.9%  |  Range: -5.83% .. +0.58%
                      Notable: ML-KEM-1024 Keypair  -5.83% (best -Os gain here)

    STACK_XTREME    : -Os is consistently SLOWER, but by a small margin.
                      Mean: +1.4%  |  Range: +0.02% .. +2.23%

    STACK           : -Os is FASTER for 512 and 768 (by ~2-3%),
                      but SLOWER for 1024 by a large margin (~9.6-10.1%).
                      Mean: +1.6%  |  Range: -3.12% .. +10.14%
                      Notable: ML-KEM-1024 Encap  +10.14% (worst -Os result)

    STACK_DUALCORE  : -Os is FASTER overall, with the strongest gains
                      on ML-KEM-512.
                      Mean: -2.3%  |  Range: -6.38% .. +1.42%
                      Notable: ML-KEM-512 Keypair  -6.38% (best -Os gain overall)

  Summary table (mean Δ% per mode, + = -Os slower, - = -Os faster):

    SPEED           : +2.2%   (-Os worse)
    SPEED_DUALCORE  : -1.9%   (-Os better)
    STACK_XTREME    : +1.4%   (-Os slightly worse)
    STACK           : +1.6%   (-Os mixed; 1024 significantly worse)
    STACK_DUALCORE  : -2.3%   (-Os better)


STACKMAX (peak stack, bytes)
----------------------------
  The vast majority of measurements are IDENTICAL between ATTR and -Os —
  stack footprint is determined almost entirely by the algorithm and mode,
  not by the compiler flag in this comparison.

  Notable exceptions:
    +8.70%   STACK_XTREME  / ML-KEM-1024 / Keypair  (-Os uses 160 bytes MORE)
    +5.69%   STACK         / ML-KEM-1024 / Keypair  (-Os uses 192 bytes MORE)
    +5.63%   STACK         / ML-KEM-1024 / Encap    (-Os uses 192 bytes MORE)
    +5.71%   STACK         / ML-KEM-1024 / Decap    (-Os uses 192 bytes MORE)
    -2.99%   SPEED         / ML-KEM-512  / Keypair  (-Os uses 192 bytes LESS)
    -2.54%   STACK_XTREME  / ML-KEM-768  / Keypair  (-Os uses  48 bytes LESS)

  All other deltas are 0.00% (identical stack usage).

  The ML-KEM-1024 STACK/STACK_XTREME outliers indicate the ATTR configuration
  achieves a tighter stack layout for the 1024-bit parameter set in those modes,
  which could be relevant for extremely constrained deployments.


CONCLUSION
----------
  The choice between ATTR and -Os depends on the target use case:

  - For single-core SPEED mode: ATTR has a consistent ~2% edge — prefer ATTR.

  - For SPEED_DUALCORE and STACK_DUALCORE: -Os is modestly faster (up to ~6%),
    particularly for smaller parameter sets — prefer -Os.

  - For STACK_XTREME: both are essentially equivalent (~1% gap); ATTR has a
    slight performance edge but -Os saves a handful of stack bytes on 512/768.

  - For STACK with ML-KEM-1024: ATTR is notably faster (~10%) and also uses
    ~192 bytes less stack — ATTR is the clear winner for this combination.

  - For STACK with ML-KEM-512 and 768: -Os is marginally faster (~2-3%).

  There is no single winner across all configurations. If a unified build flag
  must be chosen, the decision hinges on which mode and security level dominates
  the target application's workload.

